  var table = document.getElementById("excel-table");

  // Define an object to store the elements
  var elements = {};

  // Loop through each row of the table
  for (var i = 1; i < table.rows.length; i++) {
    // Get the element name, atomic number, and percentage
    var element = table.rows[i].cells[0].innerHTML;
    var atomicNumber = parseInt(table.rows[i].cells[1].innerHTML);
    var percentage = parseFloat(table.rows[i].cells[2].innerHTML);

    // Add the element to the object
    elements[element] = {
      atomicNumber: atomicNumber,
      percentage: percentage
    };
  }

  // Return the elements object
  return elements;
}
```
In this example, the function uses a `for` loop to iterate through each row of the Excel table and retrieve the element name, atomic number, and percentage. It then stores this information in an object with the element name as the key and the atomic number and percentage as the values.
To use this function, you'll need to replace `"excel-table"` with the ID of your Excel table in the `getElementById` function. You can then call the `getElementsFromTable` function to retrieve the elements from the table.

To incorporate multiple non-linear variables into a regression model using JavaScript, you can use a library like TensorFlow.js or ml.js. These libraries provide built-in functions for creating and training regression models.
