To efficiently search for multiple specific strings in a file and export the results to a new file using JavaScript, you can use the Node.js built-in `fs` (file system) module and the `grep` command.
Here's an example code snippet:
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const { exec } = require('child_process');
const filePath = '/path/to/your/file.txt';
const searchString1 = 'string1';
const searchString2 = 'string2';
const searchString3 = 'string3';
// Use the grep command to search for the strings in the file
exec(`grep -E '${searchString1}|${searchString2}|${searchString3}' ${filePath}`, (error, stdout, stderr) => {
  if (error) {
    console.error(`exec error: ${error}`);
    return;
  }
  // Write the results to a new file
  fs.writeFile('/path/to/your/newfile.txt', stdout, (err) => {
    if (err) throw err;
    console.log('Results saved to new file!');
  });
});
```
In this example, `filePath` is the path to the file you want to search in, and `searchString1`, `searchString2`, and `searchString3` are the strings you want to search for. 
The `grep` command uses the `-E` flag to enable extended regular expressions and the `|` operator to search for multiple strings. The output of the `grep` command is captured in the `stdout` variable, which is then written to a new file using the `fs.writeFile` method.
To optimize this process for large files, you can use the `createReadStream` method from the `fs` module to read the file in chunks instead of loading the entire file into memory at once. Here's an example:
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const { exec } = require('child_process');
const filePath = '/path/to/your/file.txt';
const searchString1 = 'string1';
const searchString2 = 'string2';
const searchString3 = 'string3';
// Use the grep command to search for the strings in the file
const grepCommand = `grep -E '${searchString1}|${searchString2}|${searchString3}'`;
const grepProcess = exec(grepCommand);
// Create a write stream to the new file
const writeStream = fs.createWriteStream('/path/to/your/newfile.txt');
// Pipe the grep process output to the write stream
grepProcess.stdout.pipe(writeStream);
// Log any errors
grepProcess.stderr.on('data', (data) => {
  console.error(`grep stderr: ${data}`);
});
// Log when the write stream is finished
writeStream.on('finish', () => {
  console.log('Results saved to new file!');
});
// Log any write stream errors
writeStream.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error(`Write stream error: ${err}`);
});
// Log any grep process errors
grepProcess.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error(`Grep process error: ${err}`);
});
```
In this example, the `grep` command is executed as a child process and its output is piped to a write stream. The `createReadStream` method reads the file in chunks and sends them to the `grep` process. This way, the file is not loaded into memory all at once, which can be more efficient for large files.

