Yes, it is possible to use Scala to allocate resources to multiple tasks while taking into account the critical path and limited resources. One way to approach this problem is by using a scheduling algorithm such as the Critical Path Method (CPM) or the Resource-Constrained Project Scheduling (RCPS) algorithm.
To prioritize tasks with the greatest impact on the project's completion and minimize the project's duration within the budget, you can assign weights to each task based on their importance and urgency. You can then use these weights to prioritize the tasks and allocate resources accordingly.
To handle tasks with multiple predecessors or successors, you can use a dependency graph to represent the relationships between tasks. This graph can be used to identify the critical path and ensure that all tasks are completed in the correct order.
Here is an example Scala code that implements the RCPS algorithm to allocate resources to multiple tasks:
```
import scala.collection.mutable
case class Task(id: Int, duration: Int, resources: Int, predecessors: List[Int])
def allocateResources(tasks: List[Task], budget: Int): Map[Int, Int] = {
  val taskMap = tasks.map(task => (task.id, task)).toMap
  val resourceMap = mutable.Map[Int, Int]()
  val startTimes = mutable.Map[Int, Int]()
  val endTimes = mutable.Map[Int, Int]()
  // Calculate earliest start times
  tasks.foreach(task => {
    val earliestStart = task.predecessors.map(predecessor => endTimes(predecessor)).maxOption.getOrElse(0)
    startTimes(task.id) = earliestStart
    endTimes(task.id) = earliestStart + task.duration
  })
  // Calculate latest start times
  tasks.reverse.foreach(task => {
    val latestFinish = taskMap.values.filter(_.predecessors.contains(task.id)).map(predecessor => startTimes(predecessor.id) + predecessor.duration).minOption.getOrElse(endTimes(task.id))
    startTimes(task.id) = latestFinish - task.duration
    endTimes(task.id) = latestFinish
  })
  // Allocate resources
  tasks.foreach(task => {
    val availableResources = (startTimes(task.id) to endTimes(task.id)).map(resourceMap.getOrElse(_, 0)).sum
    val requiredResources = task.resources
    val allocatedResources = if (availableResources >= requiredResources) requiredResources else availableResources
    resourceMap ++= (startTimes(task.id) until endTimes(task.id)).map((_, allocatedResources)).toMap
  })
  // Return resource allocation map
  resourceMap.toMap
}
```
This code takes a list of tasks, each with an ID, duration, required resources, and a list of predecessor task IDs. It also takes a budget parameter that represents the maximum amount of resources that can be allocated at any given time.
The algorithm first calculates the earliest and latest start times for each task based on the task durations and predecessor relationships. It then allocates resources to each task based on the available resources and required resources, while taking into account the start and end times of each task.
The function returns a map that maps each time step to the number of resources allocated at that time step. This map can be used to visualize the resource allocation over time and ensure that all tasks are completed within the budget and critical path constraints.
Note that this code is just an example and may need to be adapted to your specific use case. It also assumes that tasks can be partially allocated resources and delayed if necessary, without affecting the critical path.

